12†Our leadership L32: \nL33: Our international leadership team drives our company, guides our strategy and leads our people.\nL34: \nL35:     * cite13†Image L36: \nL37: ### cite14†Innovation and R&D L38: \nL39: Innovation is at the heart of everything we do within R&D at Unilever.\nL40: \nL41:     *       * cite15†History & Archives L42:       * cite16†Our position on L43: \nL44: cite17†View Our company L45: \nL46:   * cite18†News L47: \nL48: cite19†Skip to Brands cite5†Back L49: \nL50: ## Latest news stories\nL51: \nL52:     * cite20†Image L53: ### cite21†All news stories L54: \nL55: View our latest news, with options to search by topic or date.\nL56: \nL57:     * cite22†Image L58: \nL59: ### cite23†Press and media L60: \nL61: See the very latest from our press desk, plus media assets and contact details.\nL62: \nL63:     * cite24†Image L64: \nL65: ### cite25†Sign up for alerts from Unilever L66: \nL67: Be the first to hear about our press releases, features and articles.\nL68: \nL69: cite26†View Latest news stories L70: \nL71:   * cite27†Brands L72: \nL73: cite28†Skip to Sustainability cite19†Back L74: ## Brands\nL75: \nL76:     * ### Our Business Groups\nL77: \nL78:       * cite29†Beauty & Wellbeing L79:       * cite30†Personal Care L80:       * cite31†Home Care L81:       * cite32†Foods L82:     * cite33†Image L83: \nL84: ### cite34†Our brands L85: \nL86: Led by our Power Brands, we are meeting consumers’ daily needs, from household staples to premium indulgence.\nL87: \nL88:     * cite35†Image L89: \nL90: ### cite36†What's in our products? L91: \nL92: Discover the ingredients in our home, beauty and personal care products.\nL93: \nL94: cite37†View Brands L95: \nL96:   * cite38†Sustainability L97: \nL98: cite39†Skip to Suppliers cite28†Back L99: ## Sustainability\nL100: \nL101:     * cite40†Image L102: \nL103: ### cite41†Climate L104: \nL105: The world must move further, faster to avoid the worst effects of climate change.\nL106: \nL107:     * cite42†Image L108: \nL109: ### cite43†Nature L110: \nL111: We’re committed to contributing to the protection and regeneration of nature, within and beyond our value chain.\nL112: \nL113:     * cite44†Image L114: \nL115: ### cite45†Plastics L116: \nL117: Given the size of the plastics challenge, we’re using our innovation capabilities to find new, scalable solutions.\nL118: \nL119:     * cite46†Image L120: ### cite47†Livelihoods L121: \nL122: We’re working to improve the livelihoods of people in our global value chain.\nL123: \nL124:     * cite48†Image L125: \nL126: ### cite49†Responsible business L127: \nL128: Our progress and reporting on issues such as human rights, equity, diversity and inclusion, and safety.\nL129: \nL130: cite50†View Sustainability L131: \nL132:   * cite51†Suppliers L133: \nL134: cite52†Skip to Working at Unilever cite39†Back L135: \nL136: ## Suppliers\nL137: \nL138:     * cite53†Image L139: ### cite54†Becoming a Unilever supplier L140: \nL141: We want to work with partners who share our purpose and values. See what’s involved.\nL142: \nL143:     * cite55†Image L144: \nL145: ### cite56†Partner to Win L146: \nL147: Our Partner to Win programme is taking our partnerships to a whole new level.\nL148: \nL149:     * cite57†Image L150: \nL151: ### cite58†Living Wage Supplier Programme L152: \nL153: We champion fair pay by ensuring a living wage for all employees and partnering with suppliers to extend this commitment across our supply chain.\nL154: \nL155:     * cite59†Image L156: ### cite60†Supplier Climate Programme L157: \nL158: We're collaborating with key suppliers to reduce emissions from raw materials, ingredients and packaging by 2030, which will help us progress towards our net zero ambition.\nL159: \nL160:     *       * cite61†Terms and Conditions L161:       * cite62†How to invoice us L162:       * cite63†Report a concern L163: \nL164: cite64†View Suppliers L165: \nL166:   * cite65†Working at Unilever L167:   * cite66†Investors L168: \nL169: cite67†Back L170: \nL171: ## Investors\nL172: \nL173:     * cite33†Image L174: ### cite68†Why invest in Unilever L175: \nL176: Read about the reasons to invest in Unilever.\nL177: \nL178:     * cite69†Image L179: \nL180: ### cite70†Results and events L181: \nL182: Access information from financial announcements, presentations and events.\nL183: \nL184:     * cite71†Image L185: \nL186: ### cite72†Annual Report and Accounts 2025 L187: \nL188: Building for consistent, higher performance.\nL189: \nL190:     * cite73†Image L191: ### cite74†Investor Centre L192: \nL193: Information for shareholders on our structure and listings as well as current and historic share prices.\nL194: \nL195:     *       * cite75†Corporate governance L196:       * cite76†Regulatory announcements L197:       * cite77†Foods combines with McCormick L198:       * cite78†Contacts L199: \nL200: cite79†View Investors L201: \nL202:   * cite80†Search site L203: \nL204:   * We’ve announced a new $270 million (€234 million) digital-first, AI-powered global innovation centre in the US to accelerate scientific innovation in Beauty, Wellbeing and Personal Care\nL205:   * AI-powered digital twins in Unilever’s factories are cutting energy consumption, optimising ingredients and delivering superior quality for our products across our global manufacturing network, for example by boosting capacity by 10% at our personal care factory in Raeford, North Carolina, in the US\nL206:   * AI is speeding up delivery and increasing the impact of marketing campaigns, like Dove’s #ChangeTheCompliment, to rapidly analyse consumer responses to creative messaging and single out those that connect most with the target audience\nL207: \nL208: cite81†Image: Sam Kini, Unilever’s Chief Digital & Technology Officer Sam Kini, Unilever’s Chief Digital & Technology Officer\nL209: In this Q&A, Unilever’s Chief Digital & Technology Officer, Sam Kini, explains how Unilever is applying AI at scale across the business value chain to drive real impact for our 3.7 billion consumers.\nL210: \nL211: What defines an AI-first organisation, and what gives Unilever a competitive advantage in this new era?\nL212: Becoming AI-first is one of the three foundational pillars of Unilever’s business transformation. For us, technology is at the core of value creation, with AI as the digital backbone, helping us generate demand faster, turn data into actionable consumer insights, respond to market shifts with greater agility and unlock value company-wide.\nL213: \nL214: At Unilever, we apply AI across three distinct areas:\nL215:   * To drive growth and build the foundations for the future of commerce\nL216:   * To build an intelligent enterprise across the value chain, embedding AI across our core processes and the way we work\nL217:   * To equip our people with the right tools and make AI an essential part of everyday work\nL218: \nL219: Our competitive advantage is built on trust – in our brands, our customer relationships and our data. This gives us a strong foundation to innovate, as consumers already recognise and value what we stand for.\nL220: How is AI changing the future of commerce?\nL221: \nL222: \nL223: Some 3.7 billion people use our products every day, that’s roughly half the world’s population.\nL224: \nL225: But the traditional path to purchase is changing. AI is increasingly shaping the entire shopping journey – making the experience more agentic and conversational – and transforming how people discover, compare and buy our brands.\nL226: Research from cite82†McKinsey†www.mckinsey.com shows that 50% of shoppers use AI to discover and compare products, while cite83†Bain & Company†www.bain.com estimates that the US agentic commerce market could reach $300–500 billion (€260–433 billion) by 2030, making up roughly 15–25% of e-commerce.\nL227: \nL228: And that means rethinking the way we work, from innovation and product content to fulfilment, structured data and retailer collaboration.\nL229: To serve today’s consumers, our brands need to be designed for both people and algorithms, so they become the most trusted answer in an AI‑mediated marketplace.\nL230: \nL231: How are you bringing this AI-first strategy to life?\nL232: We’re turning intelligence into a growth engine. AI-ready data is closing the gap between insight and action, helping us generate demand faster, be more agile in our response to market shifts and create a differentiating competitive advantage that’s delivering impact across our value chain.\nL233: \nL234: 3 ways AI is driving value creation\nL235:   * AI-powered digital twins in Unilever’s factories are cutting energy consumption, optimising ingredients and delivering superior quality for our products across our global manufacturing network. At our personal care factory in the US, it’s helping boost production capacity by 10%.\nL236:   * A $270 million (€234 million) digital-first, AI-powered global innovation centre focused on Beauty, Wellbeing and Personal Care Power Brands is under development in the US. This adds to our €100 million investment to expand our in-house fragrance capabilities and £150 million (€174 million) investment in Port Sunlight.\nL237:   * AI is reinventing our marketing lifecycle, delivering insights and optimising performance by rapidly analysing consumer responses to creative messaging. Dove’s #ChangeTheCompliment campaign, for example, went live across 25 markets with 100+ pieces of content and achieved 94% positive sentiment.\nL238: How is AI reshaping how Unilever builds, measures and maintains trust?\nL239: We have iconic household brands that are part of everyday life for millions of consumers. That trust, combined with our scale, is a unique advantage as we move into the AI age. AI is not a substitute for product quality, science or consumer trust. It’s raising the bar.\nL240: Our innovation process has always started with the consumer and understanding their needs. Today, our Beauty & Wellbeing R&D teams use AI to analyse up to 1,000 data sources every month, drawn from social, search and retailers, tapping into consumer behaviours that enable product development teams to act quickly on emerging trends.\nL241: Unilever’s success has long been driven by world-class science at the heart of our brands. And today, demonstrated superiority and evidence-backed credibility matter more than ever to both humans and algorithms. We are turning strong, structured data into a machine-readable engine. For example, in Beauty & Wellbeing, our claims generation process is now 75% faster.\nL242: \nL243: What do organisations need to do to thrive in an AI-first future?\nL244: At Unilever, we are combining the pace of innovation with strong governance to deploy AI responsibly at scale.\nL245: \nL246: Success in the AI era requires both speed and care, which is why we are taking a holistic approach across our people, culture and technology on how we deploy it.\nL247: \nL248: As technology becomes widespread, it’s people and processes that become the real differentiators, underpinned by leaders who role-model this transformation.\nL249: To date, we’ve trained over 40,000 of our employees to use AI. Putting the I in AI strengthens both business performance and the way we work every day.\nL250: \nL251: The future of enterprise AI will not be defined by the technology itself. It will be defined by what we, as humans, choose to do with it. And that means preparing our organisations to scale AI with trust, speed and impact.\nL252: ## Frequently asked questions\nL253: \nL254:   * ## What does it mean to become an AI-first organisation?\nL255: \nL256: An AI-first organisation embeds artificial intelligence into core business processes, decision-making and operating models, moving beyond pilots to enterprise-wide deployment. This requires strong data and cloud foundations, scalable platforms and a workforce equipped to work alongside intelligent systems.\nL257: \nL258:   * ## What role do data and cloud infrastructure play in scaling AI successfully?\nL259: Robust data and cloud foundations are critical for scaling AI. High-quality, connected data enables accurate insights, while cloud platforms provide the flexibility and computing power needed to deploy AI solutions across geographies and functions. Together, they ensure AI can move beyond pilots to deliver consistent, enterprise-wide impact.\nL260: \nL261:   * ## What is agentic AI, and why is it important for enterprises?\nL262: Agentic AI refers to autonomous or semi-autonomous AI systems that can plan, execute and optimise complex workflows with minimal human intervention. For enterprises, it has the potential to transform productivity by orchestrating tasks across systems, reducing manual effort and enabling faster, data-driven decision-making.\nL263: ## Related articles\nL264: \nL265: cite84†Image: Modern glass-and-steel office building with Unilever logo at dusk, lit interior visible, pedestrians and cars outside on a city street. L266: ### cite85†Unilever unveils plans for $270 million US-based global innovation centre L267: \nL268: 28 May 2026\nL269: \nL270: The digital-first, AI-powered R&D facility in North America will accelerate scientific discovery and development for our beauty, wellbeing and personal care Power Brands, helping to drive growth both in the US and globally.\nL271: \nL272: cite86†Image: Three Pond’s Hydra Miracle Ultra Light products. Blue with silver accents. A bottle of serum is open, the pipette is visible. L273: ### cite87†How AI is transforming innovation in Unilever Beauty & Wellbeing L274: \nL275: 27 April 2026\nL276: \nL277: Unilever’s Beauty & Wellbeing scientists are using AI to decode consumer trends and mine decades of research data. The result? Products designed in days instead of months, and actionable insights rooted in real consumer desires. Here’s how it works.\nL278: \nL279: cite88†Image: A range of Unilever products including Hellmann’s mayo, Comfort fabric conditioner, Dove shower gel and TREsemmé shampoo. L280: ### cite89†How AI is helping drive Desire at Scale across Unilever L281: \nL282: 30 September 2025\nL283: \nL284: AI-powered content creation is fuelling a marketing transformation that is delivering growth and reach across our brands. Here are four Unilever AI case study success stories.\nL285: \nL286: cite90†Back to top L287: ## Connect with us\nL288: \nL289: We're always looking to connect with those who share an interest in our products and brands.\nL290: \nL291:   * cite91†Connect with us on Facebook†www.facebook.com L292:   * cite92†Connect with us on X†x.com L293:   * cite93†Connect with us on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com L294:   * cite94†Connect with us on Instagram†www.instagram.com L295:   * cite95†Connect with us on YouTube†www.youtube.com L296: ## Contact us\nL297: \nL298: Get in touch with Unilever PLC and specialist teams in our headquarters, or find contacts around the world.\nL299: \nL300: cite96†Contact us L301:   * cite97†What's in our products?†wiop.unilever.co.uk L302:   * cite96†Contact L303:   * cite98†Legal notice L304:   * cite99†UK Modern Slavery Act Transparency Statement L305:   * cite100†Vulnerability Disclosure Policy L306:   * cite101†Cookie Notice†www.unilevernotices.com L307:   * cite102†Privacy Notice†www.unilevernotices.com L308:   * cite103†Sitemap L309:   * cite104†Search Jobs†careers.unilever.com L310:   * cite105†Accessibility†notices.unilever.com L311:   * cite106†Digital Sustainability†notices.unilever.com L312: \nL313: cite107†Image L314: \nL315: This is Unilever's global company website\nL316: © Unilever 2026\nL317: \nL318: Cookie Settings ()\nL319: \nL320: cite108†AbilityNet†abilitynet.org